exercises.solution:
Writing the liquid fraction as $f_\text{L} = 1 - f_\text{s}$, the Scheil equation reads $C_\text{L} = C_0 f_\text{L}^{\,k-1}$. Setting $C_\text{L} = C_\text{E}$ and solving:

```python
def scheil_eutectic_fraction(C0, k, C_E):
    if not 0 < k < 1:
        raise ValueError("k must lie between 0 and 1")
    if not 0 < C0 < C_E:
        raise ValueError("C0 must lie between 0 and the eutectic composition")
    return (C_E / C0) ** (1 / (k - 1))
```

For Al–4 wt% Cu ($k = 0.17$, $C_\text{E} = 33.2$ wt%) this gives 0.078, as in the lesson, and for 2.5 wt% Cu, 0.044. Equilibrium predicts no eutectic in either alloy, because both are below the 5.65 wt% solubility limit; the difference is entirely due to the slow diffusion of copper in the solid. Real castings lie between the two models, because some back-diffusion does happen, which is why the measured amount of non-equilibrium eutectic depends on the cooling rate.

